  "worked_solution": "The area of a trapezoid is given by \n\n$$\nA=\\frac{(B+b)h}{2},\n$$\n\n where $b$ is the smaller base, $B$ is the larger base and $h$ is the height of the trapezoid.\n\nWe are given that $B=2b$ and $h=2,$ so we can express the area $A$ as a function of the base $b$ by substituting $B=2b$ and $h=2$ in the formula above. We get\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{(b+2b)(2)}{2}=3b.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate of change of $A(b)$ with respect to $b$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{\u2032}(b) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}b}(3b) \\\\  & =3\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}b}(b) \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
